@*
    VersionStamp — which build of each half is running.

    Two figures, because there are two things that can be out of date. The
    client's is a constant this app was compiled with, so it is on the page from
    the first frame. The server's is a reading taken over the wire: a Release
    deployment builds and ships the two separately, and "which API is actually
    answering" is the question somebody asks at the worst possible moment — so
    the footer answers it before they have to.

    It is fine print, and it is set as fine print. The console dresses as an
    official record, and a record's revision lives in the corner of the sheet:
    a label and a figure, the same order as the rail, at the smallest size the
    page uses. No colour of its own — it takes whatever its host's fine print is
    set in, which is what makes it legible on the navy panel and on paper without
    a rule for each. Emphasis is weight alone.

    Nothing moves. The server's figure is a still ellipsis until it arrives,
    because motion in this console answers a person's action and nothing here
    loops. And no live region: a version arriving is not news, and announcing it
    on every page load would make it some.

    Labelled in the maintainers' words on purpose. Nobody reads this line except
    whoever has been asked "what version are you on?", and the answer they need
    to give back is in terms of the two projects.
*@
@inject ServerVersionProvider VersionProvider

@* A description list with each pair in a div — the structure the rail uses, and
   for the same reason: these are names and their values, not a sentence. *@
<dl class="version">
    <div class="version__item">
        <dt class="version__label">Client</dt>
        <dd class="version__value">@ClientText</dd>
    </div>

    <div class="version__item">
        <dt class="version__label">Server</dt>
        @if (_server is null)
        {
            <dd class="version__value version__value--pending">
                <span class="sr-only">checking</span>
                <span aria-hidden="true">&hellip;</span>
            </dd>
        }
        else
        {
            <dd class="version__value">@ServerText</dd>
        }
    </div>
</dl>

@code {
    private const string ClientText = "v" + Program.Version;

    /// <summary>Null while the answer is still on its way.</summary>
    private ServerVersionReading? _server;

    /// <summary>
    /// The two refusals are worded apart because they are different faults, and only
    /// one of them is the server's. "Unreachable" is no answer at all. "Not reported"
    /// is an answer without a version in it — an API older than the endpoint, or the
    /// rate limiter — and calling that unreachable would be untrue.
    /// </summary>
    private string ServerText => _server switch
    {
        { Version: { } version } => $"v{version}",
        { Reached: false } => "unreachable",
        _ => "not reported",
    };

    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        // Asked, not waited on: the stamp has already painted with the client's figure
        // by the time this comes back. When another page has asked already, the task is
        // complete, this does not yield, and the first frame carries both.
        _server = await VersionProvider.GetAsync();
    }
}
